{"st":"LA","name":"Louisiana","seats":[{"census":1810,"seats":1},{"census":1820,"seats":3},{"census":1830,"seats":3},{"census":1840,"seats":4},{"census":1850,"seats":4},{"census":1860,"seats":5},{"census":1870,"seats":6},{"census":1880,"seats":6},{"census":1890,"seats":6},{"census":1900,"seats":7},{"census":1910,"seats":8},{"census":1920,"seats":8},{"census":1930,"seats":8},{"census":1940,"seats":8},{"census":1950,"seats":8},{"census":1960,"seats":8},{"census":1970,"seats":8},{"census":1980,"seats":8},{"census":1990,"seats":7},{"census":2000,"seats":7},{"census":2010,"seats":6},{"census":2020,"seats":6}],"events":[{"congress":12,"year":1812,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Louisiana became the 18th state on April 30, 1812. Congress gave it 1 seat in the House until the next apportionment.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, Louisiana's House delegation grew from 1 to 3 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, Louisiana kept 3 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Louisiana's House delegation grew from 3 to 4 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Louisiana kept 4 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Louisiana's House delegation grew from 4 to 5 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Louisiana's House delegation grew from 5 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 43rd Congress (1873–1875), Louisiana elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Louisiana kept 6 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Louisiana kept 6 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Louisiana's House delegation grew from 6 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Louisiana's House delegation grew from 7 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Louisiana kept 8 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Louisiana kept 8 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Louisiana kept 8 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Louisiana kept 8 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Louisiana kept 8 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Louisiana kept 8 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Louisiana's House delegation shrank from 8 to 7 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":105,"year":1995,"kind":"court","title":"United States v. Hays","text":"Challengers to Louisiana's 4th District had won in a lower court, which found it to be a racial gerrymander. The Supreme Court held that plaintiffs who did not live in the district lacked standing; a court-imposed map was later used in 1996.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/515/737","https://www.senate.mn/departments/scr/REDIST/Redsum/lasum.htm"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Louisiana kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Louisiana's House delegation shrank from 7 to 6 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Louisiana kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":119,"year":2024,"kind":"other","title":"Second majority-Black district","text":"After federal courts found the 2022 map likely violated the Voting Rights Act, the legislature passed a map in January 2024 with a second majority-Black district. It was used in the 2024 election while a challenge to it continued.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/louisiana/","https://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/louisiana-v-callais"]},{"congress":119,"year":2026,"kind":"court","title":"Louisiana v. Callais","text":"On April 29, 2026 the Supreme Court held that the Voting Rights Act did not require a second majority-Black district and that the 2024 map was an unconstitutional racial gerrymander. The governor then suspended the May U.S. House primaries.","sources":["https://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/louisiana-v-callais","https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-109_21o3.pdf","https://kmph.com/news/nation-world/louisiana-state-legislature-passes-new-congressional-map-after-supreme-court-ruling-gop-redistricting-jeff-landry-elections"]},{"congress":120,"year":2026,"kind":"mid-decade","title":"New map after Louisiana v. Callais","text":"The legislature gave final approval to a new congressional map on May 29, 2026. It keeps one majority-Black district, the 2nd, and redraws the 6th District in the Baton Rouge area. U.S. House primaries were moved to November 3, 2026, with runoffs on December 12.","sources":["https://kmph.com/news/nation-world/louisiana-state-legislature-passes-new-congressional-map-after-supreme-court-ruling-gop-redistricting-jeff-landry-elections","https://www.wwno.org/2026-08-12/39-candidates-qualify-to-run-for-louisianas-6-congressional-districts","https://lailluminator.com/2026/05/29/litigation-looms-as-louisiana-legislature-approves-new-congressional-map/"]}],"compactness":{"c012":{"LA-0":0.062},"c013":{"LA-0":0.062},"c014":{"LA-0":0.062},"c015":{"LA-0":0.062},"c016":{"LA-0":0.062},"c017":{"LA-0":0.062},"c018":{"LA-1":0.026,"LA-2":0.411,"LA-3":0.236},"c019":{"LA-1":0.026,"LA-2":0.411,"LA-3":0.236},"c020":{"LA-1":0.026,"LA-2":0.411,"LA-3":0.236},"c021":{"LA-1":0.026,"L