e redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1860 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c038":{"why":"at-large","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana sent no members to the 38th Congress (1863–1865), so the map shows only its outline and no districts.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":0},"c039":{"why":"at-large","kind":"other","text":"From the 39th Congress the historical boundary records again show districts for Louisiana, after Congresses in which it sent no members.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":0},"c040":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1866 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c041":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1868 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c043":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1870 census, which raised Louisiana's seats from 5 to 6. It was first used in the 1872 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c044":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1874 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":6},"c058":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1900 census, which raised Louisiana's seats from 6 to 7. It was first used in the 1902 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c063":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1910 census, which raised Louisiana's seats from 7 to 8. It was first used in the 1912 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":4},"c091":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1968 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":6},"c092":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1970 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c093":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1970 census, which left Louisiana with 8 seats. It was first used in the 1972 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c098":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1980 census, which left Louisiana with 8 seats. It was first used in the 1982 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c099":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1984 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c103":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1990 census, which cut Louisiana's seats from 8 to 7. It was first used in the 1992 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c104":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1994 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c105":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: United States v. Hays. Challengers to Louisiana's 4th District had won in a lower court, which found it to be a racial gerrymander. The Supreme Court held that plaintiffs who did not live in the district lacked standing; a court-imposed map was later used in 1996.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/515/737","https://www.senate.mn/departments/scr/REDIST/Redsum/lasum.htm"],"districtsChanged":7},"c108":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2000 census, which left Louisiana with 7 seats. It was first used in the 2002 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c113":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2010 census, which cut Louisiana's seats from 7 to 6. It was first used in the 2012 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c115":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Louisiana's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 2016 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":4},"c118":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2020 census, which left Louisiana with 6 seats. It was first used in the 2022 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c119":{"why":"event","kind":"other","text":"New map following: Second majority-Black district. After federal courts found the 2022 map likely violated the Voting Rights Act, the legislature passed a map in January 2024 with a second majority-Black district. It was used in the 2024 election while a challenge to it continued.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/louisiana/","https://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/louisiana-v-callais"],"districtsChanged":6},"c120":{"why":"event","kind":"mid-decade","text":"New map following: New map after Louisiana v. Callais. The legislature gave final approval to a new congressional map on May 29, 2026. It keeps one majority-Black district, the 2nd, and redraws the 6th District in the Baton Rouge area. U.S. House primaries were moved to November 3, 2026, with runoffs on December 12.","sources":["https://kmph.com/news/nation-world/louisiana-state-legislature-passes-new-congressional-map-after-supreme-court-ruling-gop-redistricting-jeff-landry-elections","https://www.wwno.org/2026-08-12/39-candidates-qualify-to-run-for-louisianas-6-congressional-districts","https://lailluminator.com/2026/05/29/litigation-looms-as-louisiana-legislature-approves-new-congressional-map/"],"districtsChanged":6}}}